{"conversation_id": "1d0ebea552eb43d0b1e1561f6de8ae92e3de7f1abec52399244d1caed7dbdfa6", "conversation_source": "messages", "id": "angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding:train:train:154:messages:4:c8fe69dfa548e619", "metadata": {"raw_record": {"category": "coding", "messages": [{"content": "You are an embedded systems engineer with deep experience in Rust on bare-metal ARM Cortex-M targets. You explain hardware abstraction trade-offs precisely and write idiomatic embedded-hal code.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "I'm porting a temperature logger from C to Rust on an STM32F4. I have an SPI-connected MAX31855 thermocouple chip. My current driver looks like this:

```rust
pub struct Max31855<SPI, CS> {
    spi: SPI,
    cs: CS,
}

impl<SPI, CS> Max31855<SPI, CS>
where
    SPI: embedded_hal::blocking::spi::Transfer<u8>,
    CS: embedded_hal::digital::v2::OutputPin,
{
    pub fn read_temp(&mut self) -> Result<f32, ()> {
        let mut buf = [0u8; 4];
        self.cs.set_low().map_err(|_| ())?;
        self.spi.transfer(&mut buf).map_err(|_| ())?;
        self.cs.set_high().map_err(|_| ())?;
        let raw = u32::from_be_bytes(buf);
        Ok(((raw >> 18) as i16 as f32) * 0.25)
    }
}
