Three things to address — your stated concern plus two bugs lurking in there.

**1. Proper error type**

Your driver should preserve the underlying error so callers can diagnose failures. Use a generic error enum:

```rust
use embedded_hal::blocking::spi::Transfer;
use embedded_hal::digital::v2::OutputPin;

#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum Error<SpiE, PinE> {
    Spi(SpiE),
    Pin(PinE),
    OpenCircuit,
    ShortToGnd,
    ShortToVcc,
    Fault,
}
```

**2. Your driver silently ignores thermocouple faults**

The MAX31855 sets bit 16 if any fault is present, with bits 0-2 specifying which. A disconnected probe will return a reading that looks valid. You must check:

```rust
impl<SPI, CS, SpiE, PinE> Max31855<SPI, CS>
where
    SPI: Transfer<u8, Error = SpiE>,
    CS: OutputPin<Error = PinE>,
{
    pub fn read_temp(&mut self) -> Result<f32, Error<SpiE, PinE>> {
        let mut buf = [0u8; 4];
        self.cs.set_low().map_err(Error::Pin)?;
        self.spi.transfer(&mut buf).map_err(Error::Spi)?;
        self.cs.set_high().map_err(Error::Pin)?;
        let raw = u32::from_be_bytes(buf);

        // Fault detection — check before interpreting temp
        if raw & (1 << 16) != 0 {
            return Err(match raw & 0b111 {
                0b001 => Error::OpenCircuit,
                0b010 => Error::ShortToGnd,
                0b100 => Error::ShortToVcc,
                _ => Error::Fault,
            });
        }

        // Sign-extend the 14-bit value properly
        let temp_raw = ((raw >> 18) & 0x3FFF) as i16;
        let signed = if temp_raw & 0x2000 != 0 {
            temp_raw | (0xC000u16 as i16)  // extend bit 13 into 14, 15
        } else {
            temp_raw
        };
        Ok(signed as f32 * 0.25)
    }
}
```

**3. The sign extension was wrong**

Your original `(raw >> 18) as i16 as f32` truncates to 16 bits but does not sign-extend the 14-bit two's complement value. A reading of -1°C (raw bits `11_1111_1111_1100`) would be interpreted as +16380 × 0.25 = 4095°C. The fix is to detect bit 13 and manually extend. Alternatively, `((raw as i32) << 14 >> 18) as f32 * 0.25` does the same via arithmetic shift.

