```rust
struct Parsed {
    source: String,
    name: &str,  // points into source
}
```

The compiler hates me. I've tried adding lifetimes every way I can think of — is this just impossible, or am I missing a trick?", "role": "user"}, {"content": "[BUTLER_THINK_START]
Classic self-referential struct. The user is hitting one of Rust's hard edges: a struct cannot safely hold a reference to data it also owns, because moving the struct (which Rust does freely) would invalidate the reference. This isn't a lifetime annotation problem — no combination of `'a` will work, because the lifetime of `name` would need to be tied to `self`, and that's not expressible in safe Rust's type system.

The options, in roughly increasing order of "idiomatic Rust":

1. **Store an index / range instead of a reference.** `name: Range<usize>` or `(start, end)`. Then `fn name(&self) -> &str { &self.source[self.name.clone()] }`. This is almost always the right answer. It's what `syn` and every mature parser crate does. The "reference" is reconstituted on demand; the struct is movable and Copy-friendly.

2. **Store an owned `String` for `name`** — allocate a copy. If `name` is small and the ergonomics win is big, this is fine. Premature optimization avoidance.

3. **`Rc<String>` + `Rc<str>` slicing** — share ownership. Overkill here but valid.

4. **`ouroboros` or `self_cell` crates** — provide macro-based self-referential structs. Work, but introduce a dependency and hide complexity. I'd recommend against unless the index approach genuinely doesn't fit.

5. **Unsafe with `Pin`** — possible but a footgun cannon. Don't.

I should lead with option 1 because it's the answer 90% of the time, explain *why* the original doesn't work (move invalidates refs), and briefly mention when you might reach for `ouroboros`. No unsafe advice.
[BUTLER_THINK_END]

Not impossible — but not expressible directly. The borrow checker is right here, and the reason is worth understanding: if `Parsed` owned its `String` and also held a `&str` into it, moving `Parsed` (which Rust does implicitly on return, assignment, push to a `Vec`, etc.) would change the `String`'s heap pointer — wait, actually it wouldn't, because `String` holds its data on the heap and the pointer is stable across moves. But Rust can't prove that in the general case, and more importantly: self-referential structs are the gateway to a whole family of soundness issues, so the type system just refuses.

The idiomatic fix is to store a *range* into the source, not a reference:

```rust
use std::ops::Range;

pub struct Parsed {
    source: String,
    name: Range<usize>,
}

impl Parsed {
    pub fn new(source: String, name: Range<usize>) -> Self {
        assert!(name.end <= source.len());
        assert!(source.is_char_boundary(name.start));
        assert!(source.is_char_boundary(name.end));
        Self { source, name }
    }

    pub fn name(&self) -> &str {
        &self.source[self.name.clone()]
    }

    pub fn source(&self) -> &str {
        &self.source
    }
}
```

`name()` returns a `&str` tied to `&self`'s lifetime, which is exactly what you wanted — the caller can hold the slice only as long as they hold the struct. The struct is freely movable, `Send`, `Sync`, Clone-friendly (if you derive it).

Two gotchas with the range approach:
- Use `assert!(source.is_char_boundary(...))` in the constructor so you panic loudly if someone passes an index splitting a UTF-8 codepoint. Without it, the `&self.source[range]` will panic later, far from the bug.
- If `Parsed` is serialized/deserialized, store the range as two `usize`s or a `(usize, usize)` tuple; `Range` isn't `Copy`, which is mildly annoying.

This is what `syn`, `rowan`, and pretty much every serious Rust parser do — they never hold slices into their own buffers.", "role": "assistant"}, {"content": "Okay the range approach works. But I'm writing a parser with dozens of these "spans," and every time I want to inspect one I have to thread the original `&source` through. It's getting gross — functions that conceptually operate on a token now all take `(&self, &source)`. Is there a cleaner pattern?", "role": "user"}, {"content": "[BUTLER_THINK_START]
