    span: Range<usize>,
}

pub struct Parser<'a> {
    arena: &'a Bump,
    source: &'a str,
}
```

All tokens borrow from the same arena and the same source string. One lifetime (`'a`) propagates through everything. This is what high-performance parsers use — it's what `swc` does. The cost is viral lifetime parameters on every type.

**The `rowan` pattern** (used by rust-analyzer) goes further: a green tree of immutable, interned nodes behind `Arc`, with red-tree views that lazily reconstruct parents. Worth studying if you're building a long-lived IDE-oriented parser, overkill for a compiler front-end.

For most parsers I'd start with `Arc<str>`. You can always migrate to an arena later; the public API shape (tokens that know their own text) doesn't change. Going the other direction — ripping out an arena after you've leaned on its lifetimes everywhere — is much more painful.", "role": "assistant"}, {"content": "Arc<str> it is. One thing bugs me though: if every Token has its own Arc, aren't I incrementing the refcount on every clone? That's an atomic op. Parsers clone tokens a lot. Am I about to performance-regress from the naive &str version?", "role": "user"}, {"content": "[BUTLER_THINK_START]
Thoughtful concern. Let me actually think about this rather than handwave.

Atomic refcount cost: on x86, `lock xadd` is ~10-20ns uncontended. On ARM with LL/SC, similar order. A parser that clones a token per call, across say 100k tokens, is 10k * 20ns = 200μs total. Probably a rounding error next to the parsing itself.

BUT: if they're cloning in tight loops (e.g., inside a Pratt parser's peek/consume cycle, or passing tokens through combinator stacks), it adds up. And refcount contention across threads is real — if the token is cloned on multiple threads simultaneously, the cache line bounces. For single-threaded parsing (the common case) this doesn't apply.

However, there's a better observation: in a parser, you don't actually need to clone the Token most of the time. You need to read it. `&Token` works fine — the borrow is tied to the tree's lifetime, which is usually what you want. Cloning is only necessary when you're *moving* a token into a new AST node, and at that point one atomic op is trivial.

And the naive `&str` version isn't actually free either — it has a lifetime that ties the caller to the source buffer. So the question isn't "is Arc slower than &str" in isolation; it's "is Arc slower than &str + lifetime threading" in the full program context. Usually no, because the Arc version doesn't need defensive clones elsewhere.

Best answer: profile. But if they don't want to profile, `Rc<str>` (non-atomic) for single-threaded parsers is essentially free. Switch to `Arc` only if you actually cross threads. And `&Token` for reads, clone only on move.

I should also mention that `Arc::clone` is cheap but not free compared to no-op, whereas cloning a `&str` is literally copying two words (pointer + length). So there IS a real difference, but it's almost always dwarfed by parsing work. And if it isn't, the arena is the right answer.
[BUTLER_THINK_END]

The concern is legitimate in principle and almost always wrong in practice. Three things to keep in mind:

**1. Most of the time you don't clone, you borrow.**

```rust
fn expect_ident(&self, tok: &Token) -> Result<&str, ParseError> {
    if tok.is_ident() { Ok(tok.as_str()) } else { Err(...) }
}
```

`&Token` costs the same as `&str` — it's a pointer. You only pay the atomic refcount when you *move* a token into a new AST node (once per token, end of story). That's a one-time 10-20ns cost on commodity x86, which is noise next to the work of parsing a single token.

**2. If you really are single-threaded, use `Rc<str>`.**

`Rc<str>` is identical to `Arc<str>` but with non-atomic refcounting. A non-atomic `inc` is a single cached instruction, indistinguishable from a register op in benchmarks. Rust will stop you from sending it across threads at compile time, which is a feature.

The only reason to pick `Arc` over `Rc` in a parser is if the resulting AST will be walked from multiple threads (e.g., parallel semantic analysis). For the parse phase itself, `Rc` is free.

**3. If profiling says it's still the bottleneck, go to the arena.**

But — and I've never seen this not be true — if atomic refcounts are your parser's bottleneck, your parser is already faster than anyone's going to notice. `rustc`'s front-end, `swc`, and `biome` all use arenas because they parse millions of lines per second and every nanosecond is visible. A domain-specific parser processing user input on an API endpoint will never see the difference.

**Rule of thumb I use:**
- Pass `&Token` for reads (which is most calls).
- Clone only when building AST nodes.
- `Rc<str>` if single-threaded, `Arc<str>` if you'll cross threads later.
- Benchmark before switching to arenas.

The real cost of `Arc<str>` you should care about isn't the refcount — it's the one extra pointer indirection per `as_str()` call versus a raw `&str`. That's a potential cache miss, and in a hot inner loop it's measurable. If `as_str()` is being called in your tokenizer's inner loop, cache the result in a local `let s = tok.as_str();` outside the loop. That's a bigger win than anything else on this list.", "role": "assistant"}], "model": "claude-opus-4-7"}, "record_metadata": {"category": "coding", "model": "claude-opus-4-7"}, "source_label": "train", "source_path": "data/hf_datasets/angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding"}, "role": "assistant", "row_index": 283, "source_dataset": "angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding", "source_dataset_group": "angrygiraffe__claude-opus-4.6-4.7-reasoning-8.7k__coding", "source_language": null, "source_text": "[BUTLER_THINK_START]
Real problem. They're building something that looks like an AST where every node has a span, and passing `source` everywhere does get ugly. This is exactly the design that crates like `rowan` (used by rust-analyzer) and `syn` have grappled with. Options:

1. **Hold the source on the root, pass it down as needed.** Typical for simple parsers. The user is already doing this and finds it gross.

2. **Intern the spans into a SourceMap-style struct that all nodes reference via an ID.** The nodes hold a `SpanId`, the SourceMap has a `get(id) -> &str`. The SourceMap is the one thing that needs to be passed around. Still pass-through, but it's one argument with a clear name instead of splitting conceptually-related things.

3. **Arc<String> in each node.** Cheap clone (refcount bump), no parameter threading. Each node becomes self-sufficient: `token.as_str()` works without context. Cost: slightly more memory (one Arc word per node), atomic refcount on clone. For a parser, usually negligible.

4. **ouroboros / self_cell** — if you really want the self-referential struct, these macros do it soundly. But given they now have many nodes, each being its own self-referential struct is heavier than Arc.

5. **Arena allocation with lifetimes tied to the arena.** `bumpalo` or a typed arena. Nodes are `&'arena Token<'arena>` and the whole parse tree shares the arena's lifetime. Excellent performance, but requires propagating `'arena` through every type.

For a practical middle ground, Arc<str> is usually the pragmatic choice. `Arc<str>` is cheaper than `Arc<String>` because it's one allocation instead of two, and slicing it is a view operation. Let me recommend that and contrast it with the arena approach for when perf matters.

I'll also gently note that the "threading `source` everywhere" pain is a symptom they should sit with — sometimes it's telling you the right answer IS to have one owner, and the functions that need to format error messages genuinely do need access. But if it's bad ergonomics, Arc<str> is the low-friction fix.
